From fa3c6f67fb2e9dca5ea41b27d8dd05ca61998c64 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 16:55:37 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(ui): resolve authored Bedrock server UI scenes

Evaluate loaded JSON UI templates, scoped variables, collection bindings
and bounded native string expressions against each server form. Preserve
original form IDs and button indices when projecting Java dialogs or
sending a negotiated native rendering scene to a client integration.
Keep pack texture bytes and nine-slice metadata in the converted pack,
and include the versioned built-in UI identity in conversion fingerprints.

Target Bedrock 1.26.51.1 and protocol 2193. Original native evaluator,
variable precedence and missing-template factory controls inform the
implementation: omit an unavailable child template while retaining its
siblings. Optional licensed UI archives are supplied at build time;
missing assets or unsupported scenes retain the ordinary form flow.
Native GPU, font, full binding-controller and animation parity remain
separate verification work. No local game installation is required.

Actual protocol2193 CubeCraft forms use typed elements rather than the
legacy buttons array. Normalize decoded forms through MCStructs and keep
full collection positions separate from button response ordinals. Four
original native decoder/click controls establish legacy precedence and
header/label/divider positions, without inferring native GPU behavior.

Preserve authored HTTPS image URLs and file-system bindings in the scene.
The client renderer loads their bytes asynchronously; packet and resource
workers do not perform network image downloads.

Normalize accepted JPEG and TGA UI textures to PNG using the existing
content decoders, retain native per-layer image priority and metadata,
and bound dimensions before decoding. Actual accepted server assets use
JPEG for Loot and gifting images; omitting them forced whole-form fallback.
Version the UI conversion identity so prior PNG-only caches are rebuilt.

Resolve explicit source extensions and their metadata to the normalized
PNG identity. Validate PNG dimensions before client decoding and permit
upper-layer replacements at the texture limit without adding entries.

Resolve native custom forms through a separately negotiated capability.
Share the same bounded core program for initial and local input-state
resolution, retaining all original response positions and null prose
slots. Expose toggle, input, scalar and step sliders, and nested dropdown
state with actual native bindings and resource language captions.

Keep the existing Java dialog available for unsupported controls, missing
assets and client rendering errors. Reuse the tracked form and dialog
construction for explicit fallback without discarding response identity.
The pinned form library lacks multiselect support, so that type retains
the ordinary flow. Native slider geometry and live custom responses remain
separate verification work.

Retain native slider and thumb named state subtrees while their inactive
computed visibility remains false. Original native visibility setters
activate authored hidden hover and locked branches, so pruning their
descendants loses required captions, textures and bindings. Preserve
each descendant's own bound visibility separately for selected-state
activation without overriding unrelated hidden controls or input indices.

Pinned native slider fixtures pass 184 controls for geometry, pointer
coordinates, state selection, progress clipping and value publication.
These CPU fixtures do not establish native GPU or coupled input dispatch.

Share one JsonUiProgram for form and container definitions, scoped bindings,
named collections, factories, textures and language data. FormBindings
retains original response ordinals and custom input state; ContainerUi
binds native chest collections to actual Java menu slot identities.
Distinguish singleton factories from repeating grid factories and retain
outer scope when selecting another collection explicitly.

Deliver bounded native_container programs only after the actual Java
OPEN_SCREEN and only under its negotiated capability. Keep connection-local
presentation sequencing independent of inventory revisions and requests.
Java content, cursor, close and click packets remain authoritative. Retain
raw available title conditions separately from the displayed title without
inferring names from held items. Unsupported families and programs keep
the original screen; frontend rendering failures retain its ordinary menu.

Targeted production codec/packet tests verify both chest sizes, actual
packet order, repeated menu IDs, capability fallback and payload bounds.
Private target 1.26.51.1 definitions and the accepted server pack resolve
and round-trip every 3-row and 6-row slot with no missing templates.
Native actor title/size, transient inventory renderers, coupled input and
native pixels remain verification boundaries, not parity claims.

Retain typed count-label provenance in container scenes from authored text
and binding sources, including variable aliases and selected cursor counts.
Expose the original merged control to the domain decorator without changing
form actions. An unrelated later text binding clears the count annotation.
The frontend can draw real Java stack counts once at that authored geometry
while retaining unrelated numeric labels; no control-name heuristic is used.

Preserve authored members when a higher resource-pack layer partially
overrides the same namespace and control name. Keep an existing explicit
parent until an upper declaration supplies another parent, and retain
top-level replacement for controls and binding arrays.

Versioned target UI and accepted server inputs contain partial count,
chest-title, keyboard-image and text-entry-label declarations without a
type. Whole-definition replacement erased their lower type and bindings.
This layering behavior is inferred from those target inputs; original
native definition-store merge execution remains separate verification.
Four regression cases and actual three/six-row scene probes retain the
63/90 native count labels, title labels and real collection addresses.

Keep known inactive action/custom controller collections empty when both
controllers occur in the authored screen. Empty counterpart length bindings
prevent factories from manufacturing rows of the wrong form type. Preserve
unknown-collection rejection, real input state and original response indices.

Select actor-owned chest titles from retained typed NAME and nonempty
FILTERED_NAME metadata at container open. The pinned native actor getter
and metadata reader execute twelve controls for precedence, type rejection,
removal admission and empty-name dispatch. Initial scene publication sets
actor title localization separately from the untouched condition string.

Share one opening title result between Java OPEN_SCREEN and the exported
native program. Retain raw title and localization globals on program import
so authored conditions never use an already translated Java caption.
Keep physical Java custom-name translation and default captions unchanged.
Empty actor class titles, size admission, metadata updates after opening,
and actual Social actor metadata delivery remain verification boundaries.

Resolve manual grid collection ordinals before descendant bindings. The
pinned native GridItem writer uses columns * row + column, and its lookup
precedes explicit CollectionItem indices while retaining unrelated scopes.
Ignored controls do not consume addresses; malformed, overflowing and
unavailable actual collection entries keep the ordinary fallback.

Sixteen original index controls and six fresh native layout controls bind
these rules for Bedrock 1.26.51.1/protocol2193. Complex Java regressions
cover sparse and hidden controls, nested collections, conflicting indices,
round trips and invalid positions without allocating declared capacity.
Authored actor-menu subsets still require a separate frontend admission
review; this change does not fabricate slots or actor metadata delivery.

Negotiate authored manual container action scope separately from legacy
presentation bytes. Preserve referenced slot identities, bounded typed
custom-name/lore hover text and connection-local activation ownership.
The genuine server-authoritative request adapter remains in deferred
inventory work; portable UI has no request-engine compile dependency.

Bedrock 1.26.51.1 original item text and count-one planner controls justify
the scoped semantics. Controlled real-item CPU projection is separate
from actual actor-backed Social delivery, native pixels and server action
acceptance. Unknown item text retains ordinary fallback.

Resolve metadata-selected acquired TTF families to isolated Java font resources with default glyph fallback. Normalize EM size and baseline against pinned native hhea metrics; unsupported formats keep existing font fallback. Carry family IDs through scene transport without requiring a local game installation.

Accept server forms that replace an inventory, closing the container through its normal pending acknowledgement path before publishing the form. Native protocol-2193 CubeCraft Friends and Parties deliver the form before container close; treating any open inventory as UserBusy made both buttons inert. Keep busy-form rejection and prevent the late inventory acknowledgement from dismissing its replacement.

Native 1.26.51.1 TrueType rendering applies a 1.5 display transform after
hhea cache normalization. Preserve that transform in exported glyphs and
baselines, and transport fractional family line advances to the client.
Original native CPU drawing and metric getters establish the scale and
truncation; acquired MinecraftTen glyph comparisons verify the conversion.
Advance the conversion cache identity so prior font outputs are rebuilt.

Load UI image bindings from the accepted pack texture namespace, not only
textures/ui. A protocol2193 CubeCraft capture binds the profile background
and border to textures/cubecraft/profile paths; the pinned native client
renders both images. Preserve bounded decoding, format and layer priority,
and invalidate converted caches that omitted those resources.

Keep the image index bounded at 16,384 entries. The pinned built-in layer
and CubeCraft pack contain 10,127 converted UI images, so the former
8,192-entry ceiling discarded ordinary UI frames when other texture
namespaces were admitted. Keep upper-layer replacements valid at the cap.

Resolve native NPC conversations through the shared JSON UI controller.
Load the NPC host from resource packs even when their UI index omits it,
retain the student button collection and original sparse action indices,
and carry actor identity, skin-list order and portrait offsets from server
metadata. Resolve controller-bound grid dimensions before client layout.
Unknown collections and invalid dimension pairs remain explicit errors.

Transport negotiated native scenes and matching closes with a bounded,
protocol-versioned capability, retaining the existing authoritative NPC
response path. Entity removal and client responses close the same scene
identity; clients without this capability keep the Java conversation.

Pinned Bedrock 1.26.51.1 build 51061372/protocol 2193 native BDS captures
establish the default speech bubble, portrait metadata, three-column
action grid and entry/exit request lifecycle. Focused tests cover default
definitions, pack-selected layouts, nested scopes, URL/command separation,
wire limits and both negotiated and ordinary packet routes. The add-on
conversation screen, actor portrait drawing, teacher/editor controller
and direct/ViaProxy visible and interaction comparisons remain unfinished.

Derive Java UI texture identities from the case-sensitive native basename.
Pinned NPC definitions use uppercase Banners, ScrollRail and ScrollHandle
paths, which are valid Bedrock resources but invalid Java identifiers.
Keep extension normalization, metadata lookup and layer precedence at the
source path; map resolved references and converted bytes to the same digest.
Invalidate previous converted packs and test case-distinct pixel resources.

Resolve separately negotiated native NPC teacher scenes through the same
JSON UI program. Retain actor metadata, permission and effective creative
mode gates, appearance pages, dialogue editing, advanced action rows and
maximized command editing. Share stable action input identities between
initial projection and local controller updates; deletion retires old IDs.

Pinned native build 51061372/protocol 2193 on offline BDS sends SetName
and SetInteractText incrementally, SetSkin as its list index, and complete
SetActions at advanced exit with an empty scene. It emits command lines
with cmd_ver 52 and terminal LF, uses exclusive modes 0/button, 1/exit and
2/enter, and navigates Escape back through nested editor views. Retail
omits Add URL. Teacher opening and exit do not execute student commands.
Keep the student capability independent from editor support so older
add-ons cannot receive an unsupported teacher program.

Targeted tests cover typed wire responses, inherited game modes, student
isolation, action identity, text limits, skin paging, unknown action fields
and all four authored editor views. Education/WorldBuilder authority,
URL editing semantics, controller input and actual direct/proxy native
rendering remain parity verification work, not coverage claims.

NPC teacher skin collections select each actual variant and the actor's
picker_offsets camera data, distinct from conversation portrait_offsets.
Native build 51061372 exposes both transforms in actor metadata. The
private direct client confirms independently textured variants; full
picker geometry and display verification remain separate checks.

Choose NPC picker offsets only inside the skins collection. The dialogue
editor shares the student portrait camera; applying picker offsets there
clips the head, unlike the pinned native client.

Resolve title factories from accepted HUD definitions instead of exposing control markers and subtitle image paths as Java title text. Apply native array insertions before selecting factory branches, retain title/subtitle ordering, and carry bounded fade timing to clients that advertise the HUD channel. The accepted CubeCraft 0.0.55 HUD on target Bedrock 1.26.51.1 supplies the authored banner, icon binding and fade/wait/fade chain.

Prepare the shared JSON UI program only when a UI consumer needs it. Native actor and equipment storages should not repeatedly decode unrelated UI textures during loading.

Retain each active title's timing when Times configures later titles.
Native 1.26.51.1 (protocol 2193) keeps a 100-tick title visible for the
same lifetime after a mid-title Times update to 20 ticks. The next title
uses the shorter duration. Capture frame pacing affects wall-clock
duration, so compare this sequence with the fresh-title control.

Snapshot timing when the title factory starts. Do not emit a replacement
scene for Times. Subtitle updates reuse that snapshot so a later timing
configuration cannot truncate or change the active animation chain.
The regression test covers timing changes, subtitle refreshes, the next
title, and negative fields that preserve the other configured durations.

Resolve the bundled HUD title factory when no server override exists.
Target 1.26.51.1 default title and subtitle definitions are present in the
versioned UI archive, and their factory resolves without missing templates.
The prior customization guard routed this valid native scene to Java
titles despite the negotiated add-on capability. Keep the ordinary Java
route for clients without that capability and genuinely unresolved scenes.

A regression test uses the bundled definitions with no server packs and
checks cached subtitle ordering, title activation, default duration and
finite animation endpoints. Supply the native default title background
opacity (0.6), shadow preference and initial subtitle visibility; without
these controller variables, background tracks contained null endpoints
and the add-on discarded the title at render time. The native target
profile records gfx_hud_text_background_opacity:0.6. Client preference
synchronization remains separate incomplete work.
Native pixels, font metrics and separate subtitle animation remain
verification boundaries.

Resolve the bundled and pack-authored action-bar factory through the same
bounded HUD program, with a separately negotiated animation-managed lifetime.
Plain and text-object captions keep their original content. Empty packets
preserve the current caption; title Clear, Reset and Times do not reset it.
Older add-ons and ordinary clients retain their existing translation.

Native build 51061372/protocol 2193 defines three-second in-expo destruction.
An overridden six-second constant-alpha caption remains past three seconds;
a one-second override without destruction remains past nine seconds. Native
execution confirms empty plain and text-object packets preserve that caption.
Direct and ViaProxy replay screenshots verify six-second drawing, expiry,
replacement and preservation through Clear, Reset and an empty text object.
The replay's unrelated missing-world actor checks still fail; these captures
verify HUD lifecycle only. Full core tests and Checkstyle pass, with targeted
codec, payload and separate old/new capability checks using pinned UI assets.
General animation events, loops, full HUD scaling, resource reload comparison
and synchronized client opacity preferences remain unfinished parity work.

Transport resolved animation graphs with stable indices, namespace-relative
successors and shared cyclic references. The pinned 1.26.51.1 UI assets
contain looping alpha/wait chains; flattening them rejects valid controls
and loses their event lifecycle. Bound distinct tracks rather than chain
length, and keep optional missing references local to their branch.

Targeted UI resolution tests cover shared roots and cycles. Native build
51061372 animation-manager execution confirms successor reuse and same-frame
carry. Other visual animation types remain an add-on parity requirement.

Resolve offset and size property animation references alongside alpha,
retaining typed endpoints and one root per shared definition. The pinned
1.26.51.1 assets use relative vector references on valid controls. Regression
tests cover property roots and explicit animation lists without duplicate
updates. Other visual animation types remain unresolved parity work.

Resolve supported persistent authored HUD root decorationsin accepted packs, including alternate UI filenames and inherited layout. Skip vanilla controls, title/actionbar factories and unsupported dynamic bindings. Transport root scenes separately from timed overlays; bounded scene and layout tests cover supported static decorations.

Resolve color animation references and run four-channel image-component
tracks through the shared forms, HUD and container renderer. Keep easing
overshoot until final color submission, multiply image color alpha by
control opacity once, and retain color clocks across binding refreshes.

The pinned Bedrock 1.26.51.1 build51061372 color updater at 144578f30
interpolates image RGBA independently; its reset callback 1445791a0
restores the starting color. An absent image component finishes without
consuming time. Factory/parser 14457d220/14040e170 uses white defaults,
including omitted array channels, and native named color constants.

A private comparison executes the unchanged native updater across 768
frames covering all 32 easing selectors, boundary times, zero duration,
negative channels and overshoot. Production output differs by at most
1.1920929e-7. Targeted tests cover resets, successors, refreshes and final
opacity composition. This closes the image color-track implementation
gap; full UI family and native/direct/proxy visual verification remains.

Resolve image UV animation property roots alongside alpha, size, offset
and color without duplicating an explicitly listed track. Preserve the
original animation graph and source-pixel endpoints for the native add-on.
The pinned UI bundle contains 34 flip_book tracks and one UV track; many
flipbooks enter through a control UV property rather than its anims list.

Resolve Aseprite texture sidecars into atlas dimensions and complete frame
sequences before transporting authored UI programs. Preserve native image
metadata precedence over ordinary base-size and nine-slice properties.
The target requires meta.image and numeric atlas/frame fields, ignores trim
and rotation flags, and discards the whole sequence after an invalid frame.
Missing or empty frames retain the authored atlas dimensions.

Pinned Bedrock 1.26.51.1 build 51061372 metadata loader 140406370, executed
without instruction changes, establishes thirteen valid and invalid parser
cases. Production output matches every case. Targeted tests also verify
program export/replay, ignored flags and malformed metadata. This supplies
the add-on animation path without a local game installation or extra asset
fetches. Complete UI-family and direct/proxy visual verification remains.

Describe chest, ender chest, shulker box, barrel, hopper, dispenser and
dropper scenes through explicit host, panel and slot-count identities.
These are the native JSON UI roots in Bedrock 1.26.51.1 build 51061372.
Export redstone overrides even when a pack omits an explicit _ui_defs entry.
Negotiate the added families on native_container_v3; versions 1 and 2
retain their two-family domain and reject additional kinds before writing.
Tests resolve every bundled family and retain all slot addresses through
program export and replay. Native visual and route verification remains.

Resolve native #alpha and #propagateAlpha bindings on each control and restore control-local defaults for descendants. The saved target CubeCraft HUD uses these bindings. Bump the converted resource identity so existing caches do not retain omitted opacity values.

Execute authored count-one chest actions on client-authoritative servers
through normal InventoryTransaction packets. Preserve both before/after
slot states and native hotbar-first merge selection without requiring
modern stack network IDs or waiting for ItemStackResponse. Publish the
Java prediction after transport succeeds; subsequent server contents and
slot packets remain authoritative. Bind activation to the current menu
and exported presentation, and block ordinary Java clicks while active.

Share the native runtime-ID registry, immutable item definitions and
constructor-owned stack limits with the authoritative inventory adapter.
Keep request/response handling in its deferred patch. This also moves the
common presentation-channel handler and lifetime cleanup to the UI owner.

Research targets official Bedrock 1.26.51.1 build51061372 protocol2193,
executable SHA256537c0aee2e79afbdc94b44b28e00f466ae62bc50e2733d953b430db9dbaa9ee7.
Native normal wrapper14266bd30, outgoing body1427324a0 and packet14272c690
preserve constructor-owned container/player actions and balanced lifetime
cleanup. Existing native return and factory controls establish the shared
selection/limit inputs. These bounded controls do not establish the full
Player/controller sender, negotiated authority flag, serialized native
bytes or live server acceptance. The initial client-authoritative adapter
handles authored 27/54-slot chest presentations; other native providers
remain separate implementation work.

Keep the stable-registry full query API for cooking output queries,
including wildcard auxiliary values and query-sided Block identity.
Share its numeric userdata comparison with occupied-slot merge eligibility.
The cooking transfer helper 14c54c010 calls full query comparator14248dac0;
that does not establish the ordinary authored container destination rules.

Extend authored client-authoritative count-one actions to the ordinary
five-slot hopper and nine-slot dispenser/dropper providers. Move those
storage definitions and their opening/presentation foundation to this
first consumer, retaining modern request slot names in the inventory
adapter. Share exact provider/type/size and presentation-kind validation
between both inventory modes rather than maintaining chest-only gates.
An actor-resolved HopperContainer can retain generic CONTAINER on the
wire; accept that five-slot provider without changing its close identity.

Pinned 1.26.51.1 redstone UI definitions use container_items with 5x1
hopper and 3x3 dispenser/dropper grids, through the common container-item
controls. Reuse ordinary balanced normal transactions and direct external
slot identities. The graphical/controller and live server paths remain
unverified for these new client-authoritative families. Tests execute
current authored bindings, activation, merge debits/credits, family codec
negotiation and rejection of mismatched providers and workstation layouts.

Use the native occupied-slot merge predicate for authored count-one
transfers. Destination durability, typed Unbreakable data and constructed
stack limits determine stackability; subtype items compare exact effective
auxiliary values, while ordinary items ignore auxiliary differences.
Retain native userdata, restriction and blocking-state comparisons rather
than Java object equality. Share the userdata comparator with later
recipe queries, keeping their full transfer identity check separate.

Pinned 1.26.51.1/build 51061372/protocol 2193 registers auto-place-one in
state 12. Its entry 144e4bf10 reaches 144dfb400 and virtual auto-placement;
base 1433eb800 plans through 1433e9e90/1433eab10. Occupied capacity reaches
147c98ca0 through 1431b69d0 and calls 142488fe0 source-first. Complete
native 142488fe0/142488c90 controls match 756 component-backed cases with
real construction, registry ownership, binary NBT loading and cleanup.
The prior raw planner fails the native destination-selection regression.

The native controls use standalone ComponentItem 259 without associated
Block, restrictions or charged children. Effective block auxiliary uses
the existing canonical descriptor resolver; unresolved subtype metadata
cannot establish occupied-slot compatibility. Registry replacement
lifetimes, restriction hash collisions, specialized Item overrides and
complete controller/wire/live behavior remain unverified.

Require the converted resource pack to be loaded on the Java client before
publishing a native container program. A negotiated payload channel alone
does not make its hashed images available. Without those resources, the
client skips every missing panel image while still drawing inventory items.
Keep the ordinary menu until resource loading succeeds, without consuming
a presentation sequence. A packet regression fails before this guard and
passes after it, then proves the first loaded presentation still uses
sequence one. This identifies a missing-background failure path, not the
confirmed cause of the reported WildMC screenshot.

Resolve custom item names through the constructed registry factory class.
Use the shared unfiltered selector for Java CUSTOM_NAME so an empty or
wrongly typed display.Name on a native written book falls back to title.
A present but wrongly typed title selects empty custom text. Component
replacements keep the base custom-name dispatch even if a later entry
uses legacy flags. Removed runtime-ID owners do not acquire names.

The original 1.26.51.1 executable predicate143f6d580 and getter143f6d2c0
confirm title/custom-name precedence in 1,000 typed controls. Existing
native registry lifecycle controls establish the ordered factory policy.
Targeted tests compose that policy with book naming across first and
subsequent Legacy/DataDriven/None updates. This does not implement all
ordinary key families, secondary labels, native language selection or
the authored search field.

Resolve ComponentItem description keys from registry construction and ordered
component readers, rather than the join handler's entry-version filter. All
explicit construction precedes updates; an existing component receives None
and Legacy entries as well. Late fallback construction ignores earlier
metadata. Keep the real builtin component description separate from factory
version flags, and retain its observed default until explicit reconstruction.

Use these keys for mapped and unmapped Java items, preserving native naming
through the shared language engine without changing unrelated component
loaders. Original native controls cover 23 final descriptions and their class
selection. The old packaged converter fails twelve of twenty applicable
component cases. Five deliberate constructor, version, fallback, empty-value
and builtin-default regressions fail focused behavioral assertions. Full
ordinary-key providers, secondary labels, client asset precedence and cooking
search transport remain separate work.

Retain constructor-owned ordinary keys for 559 builtin definitions across 14
receiver methods that ignore descriptors and userdata. Use these keys in
production item conversion through the current native language engine.
Explicit generic component construction replaces this dispatch before
ordered component name readers; later flags cannot select another class.
Block, potion and other descriptor-dependent methods remain separate
providers rather than inheriting a default-key snapshot.

A registered legacy factory can upgrade to a block mapping without a current
block-state set. Preserve ordinary naming and the existing missing-visual
fallback in that case instead of dereferencing an absent state collection.

Dispatch thirteen descriptor-dependent name methods from their actual
constructor descriptions and variants for 41 builtin definitions. Preserve
typed NBT presence, signed-short metadata, low-byte and color masks, fish
bucket precedence, exploration labels and book title selection. Runtime
conversion supplies stack metadata and userdata through the same registry
identity and component-reconstruction checks as invariant names.

Use a shared name strategy for invariant and descriptor methods rather than
leaving a parallel default-key path. Block, potion and compound providers
still require their separate native state, language and recipe owners.

Compose descriptor-owned ordinary names with the typed native append path through the current language translator and argument formatter. Keep Java custom-name overrides separate from the underlying ordinary component, and share the same append implementation with the lazy primary selector. Native selector/I18n composition controls cover complete format failure and token expansion rather than only a deterministic lookup boundary.

Retain constructor-owned block name providers and compiled block-definition
keys separately from runtime IDs and Java display mappings. Resolve the
actual auxiliary block state before selecting the name. Preserve native
empty-name, leaf, wool/carpet and sapling fallback decisions, and retain
component reconstruction and registry ownership as dispatch boundaries.
Keep block lookup lazy so other item methods do not acquire a world-source
dependency. Potion and arrow version ownership remains separate work.

Resolve potion, splash and lingering names through the actual constructor
mode, compiled potion registry fields and current native language formatter.
Retain missing, empty and unchanged-translation fallback decisions. These
receivers share the lazy connection-owned name context with block names;
component reconstruction and runtime-ID ownership continue to gate dispatch.

Use a separate native BaseGameVersion value read from StartGame level settings.
Its parser preserves partial versions, wildcard and beta, strips accepted
suffixes before feature comparisons, and rejects malformed or overflowing
16-bit components. The existing loose session conversion differs on eighteen
of thirty-six original parser/comparator inputs and cannot drive these gates.
The original StartGame handler copies this version member into the fifth
client configure argument; bounded original copy/dispatch controls cover all
thirty-six parsed records. The complete packet decoder and client lifecycle
remain outside those native controls.

Resolve the compiled ArrowItem receiver through the same native potion
registry. Auxiliary zero delegates to the ordinary constructor description;
other descriptors select potion auxiliary minus one, with the native empty
arrow fallback. Preserve absent descriptors and raw description bytes. The
version source stays lazy for ordinary arrows, and this receiver does not
request block or language callbacks. Component reconstruction and runtime
owner removal still retire builtin dispatch.

Resolve compound primary keys from the target's 38 compiled CompoundType names.
Previously, unmapped compound metadata inherited the Salt name from the Java
carrier mapping. That changes tooltip text and the names used by native search.
Preserve the unsigned-byte auxiliary conversion, absent-descriptor value 255,
fixed native prefix and empty suffix outside the compiled enum table.

The original alias initializer registers sugar, ink sac and charcoal. Their
recipe hashes are disjoint from every 16-bit auxiliary value of the actual
compound constructor. Keep this provider bound to that constructor and retain
component reconstruction and current runtime-owner admission before dispatch.
The general cross-item compound classifier remains a separate native concern.
